1774198984195232	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEtnAE		item.update	bn-1jm	{"field":"description","value":"Investigate how the gltf crate (v1.4.1) exposes skin data: inverse bind matrices, joint hierarchy, joint indices/weights per vertex. Determine what accessor types are used and how to extract them. Check if the crate handles sparse accessors for joint data. Document the data flow from gltf::Skin → joint tree.\n\nOutput: Write findings to notes/research-gltf-skin-data.md covering:\n- API surface: key types/methods for accessing skin, joints, weights\n- Accessor layout for JOINTS_0 and WEIGHTS_0 (component types, counts)\n- Inverse bind matrix extraction\n- Joint parent-child relationship traversal\n- Sparse accessor support (yes/no, implications)\n- Code snippets showing the extraction pattern"}	blake3:plP9ZWI931GfqHWMTBJaaMUWKDbGCYQZIB460pkCsOA
1774198985887912	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEtnQE		item.update	bn-1sr	{"field":"description","value":"Investigate how the gltf crate exposes animation data: channels, samplers, keyframe times, output values (translation/rotation/scale). Understand the three interpolation modes (Step, Linear, CubicSpline). Determine buffer layout for rotation quaternions vs translation vec3s. Check how multiple animations are indexed.\n\nOutput: Write findings to notes/research-gltf-animation.md covering:\n- API surface: Animation, Channel, Sampler types and iteration\n- How to read keyframe timestamps and output values from accessors\n- Data layout per interpolation mode (especially CubicSpline tangent packing)\n- Quaternion normalization requirements\n- How multiple animations are distinguished (by name/index)\n- Code snippets showing the extraction pattern"}	blake3:vbkgcCmtefi_Mz4w7K8KukDTYUwV7MlKosA6qUbQS2s
1774198988172694	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEtngE		item.update	bn-2jm	{"field":"description","value":"Study the BVH file format: HIERARCHY section (joint names, offsets, channels) and MOTION section (frame time, channel data). Evaluate existing Rust BVH crates (bvh_anim, etc.) for suitability. Identify the key challenge: mapping BVH joint names/hierarchy to glTF skeleton joints for retargeting. Determine MVP scope — matching skeletons only, or basic name-based retargeting.\n\nOutput: Write findings to notes/research-bvh-format.md covering:\n- BVH format spec summary (hierarchy + motion sections)\n- Channel ordering conventions (Xposition, Yposition, Zposition, Xrotation, etc.)\n- Euler angle convention (which axes, intrinsic vs extrinsic)\n- Rust crate evaluation: bvh_anim and alternatives (API quality, maintenance, license)\n- Recommendation: use existing crate vs hand-roll parser\n- Joint name mapping challenge: common naming conventions across BVH sources (CMU, Mixamo, etc.)\n- MVP scope recommendation with justification"}	blake3:fLFHdqgh0sXFu5PXKh2_vZUyLNboLElVHDhapf2adk0
1774198990647396	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEtnwE		item.update	bn-l2s	{"field":"description","value":"Determine practical limits for joint palette uniforms in wgpu. A typical skeleton has 50-150 joints × 64 bytes (mat4) = 3.2-9.6 KB. Check maxUniformBufferBindingSize on common backends (Vulkan, Metal, DX12). Decide between uniform buffer vs storage buffer for the joint matrices. Check if the current bind group layout needs restructuring.\n\nOutput: Write findings to notes/research-wgpu-joint-buffer.md covering:\n- maxUniformBufferBindingSize across backends (Vulkan, Metal, DX12, WebGPU)\n- Uniform buffer vs storage buffer: performance tradeoffs, alignment requirements\n- Recommendation: which buffer type to use and max joint count\n- Current msh bind group layout (group indices, what's bound where)\n- Proposed bind group changes needed for the joint palette\n- Any wgpu version-specific considerations (we're on wgpu 27)"}	blake3:kYXKOAJFzXgWEVNz3Y8eIbSQ17rv0asS9icgum9Ld-Y
1774199116324927	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEtoAE		item.update	bn-1jm	{"field":"description","value":"Investigate how the gltf crate (v1.4.1) exposes skin data: inverse bind matrices, joint hierarchy, joint indices/weights per vertex. Determine what accessor types are used and how to extract them. Check if the crate handles sparse accessors for joint data. Document the data flow from gltf::Skin → joint tree.\n\nSpike: Write a small standalone example (notes/spikes/gltf-skin-spike.rs or inline in the notes) that loads a skinned GLB and prints: joint hierarchy with parent indices, inverse bind matrices, and per-vertex joint indices + weights for the first mesh primitive. Use a test asset (find a small skinned GLB like the Khronos RiggedSimple.glb sample).\n\nOutput: Write findings to notes/research-gltf-skin-data.md covering:\n- API surface: key types/methods for accessing skin, joints, weights\n- Accessor layout for JOINTS_0 and WEIGHTS_0 (component types, counts)\n- Inverse bind matrix extraction\n- Joint parent-child relationship traversal\n- Sparse accessor support (yes/no, implications)\n- Code snippets showing the extraction pattern\n- Spike results: what worked, what was surprising, any API quirks"}	blake3:UW-W-ulfM0zGP_dNs2scAXRhng_4oe2iJ2Zz-T3ZQyQ
1774199117076888	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEtoQE		item.update	bn-1sr	{"field":"description","value":"Investigate how the gltf crate exposes animation data: channels, samplers, keyframe times, output values (translation/rotation/scale). Understand the three interpolation modes (Step, Linear, CubicSpline). Determine buffer layout for rotation quaternions vs translation vec3s. Check how multiple animations are indexed.\n\nSpike: Write a small standalone example (notes/spikes/gltf-animation-spike.rs or inline in the notes) that loads an animated GLB and prints: animation names, channel targets (joint index + property), keyframe timestamps, and sample values for a few frames. Use a test asset (Khronos RiggedSimple.glb or similar).\n\nOutput: Write findings to notes/research-gltf-animation.md covering:\n- API surface: Animation, Channel, Sampler types and iteration\n- How to read keyframe timestamps and output values from accessors\n- Data layout per interpolation mode (especially CubicSpline tangent packing)\n- Quaternion normalization requirements\n- How multiple animations are distinguished (by name/index)\n- Code snippets showing the extraction pattern\n- Spike results: what worked, what was surprising, any API quirks"}	blake3:tCdrkItEU0j9ZsN0u8OfsVpy2TXghxtEbK-DP09Cu5A
