1774206722566703	command-lattice-dev	itc:v3:AWKTkyTOeZ5M5QAAAAAAQa61bvb1ugAAAAEvDg		item.create	bn-2xs	{"description":"GLB files can contain multiple animation clips via NLA tracks (or multiple glTF animations). The viewer currently only plays the first animation. Support selecting which clip to play.\n\nContext: The command-lattice asset pipeline exports a single GLB with multiple procedural animations (idle, walk, work, death) as separate NLA tracks via Blender's glTF exporter with export_nla_strips=True / export_animation_mode='NLA_TRACKS'.\n\nAcceptance criteria:\n- msh view model.glb lists available animation clips on load\n- Keyboard shortcut (e.g. 1-9 or left/right arrows) to switch between clips\n- msh view model.glb --animation walk selects a clip by name\n- msh render model.glb --animation walk --out walk.png renders a specific clip\n- Falls back to first clip if none specified (current behavior)","kind":"task","title":"Support multiple animation clips in GLB viewer"}	blake3:VYUDjKpa7KhMHKS95unjHoDLWX9iTE5oyJ4QTPVKnJQ
1774206900177869	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEtvwE		item.move	bn-2xs	{"state":"done"}	blake3:1_rXzhaH7YjugPegsxEeDOhgqTT1TlRNmXBP_muEG2s
1774207208030297	command-lattice-dev	itc:v3:AWKTkyTOeZ5M5QAAAAAAQa61bvb1ugAAAAEvDw		item.create	bn-ivu	{"description":"Add a uniform scale multiplier flag to msh render and msh view.\n\nUsage:\n  msh render model.glb --scale 50 --out sprite.png\n  msh view model.glb --scale 50\n\nApplies a uniform scale transform to the loaded mesh and skeleton before rendering/viewing. Does not modify the file. This is needed because AI-generated models (TRELLIS, UniRig) output meshes at ~1 unit tall, while the sprite-bake camera preset expects models at game scale (~50-100 units).\n\nAuto-fitting is explicitly not wanted — different unit types need precise relative sizing.","kind":"task","title":"Add --scale flag to render and view commands"}	blake3:bPh2ymbmPUs9xq8h5Hdc-0px6-6km33jUy5mFM7MuaY
1774207583793984	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEtwAE		item.move	bn-ivu	{"state":"done"}	blake3:VzEdP73_2sDHMih0ZUJzRFAjmDfC4I5dheeaNUq9cME
1774207611309884	command-lattice-dev	itc:v3:AWKTkyTOeZ5M5QAAAAAAQa61bvb1ugAAAAEvEA		item.create	bn-2mc	{"description":"Support a step value in the --frames range syntax for skipping frames during sprite sheet rendering.\n\nSyntax: --frames start-end:step (e.g. --frames 0-39:4 renders frames 0,4,8,12,...,36)\n\nThis is needed because procedural animations are generated at 20fps for smooth playback, but sprite sheets for a 2D RTS only need 8-12 frames per animation. Rather than re-generating animations at lower frame counts, the renderer should just sample every Nth frame.\n\nExamples:\n  msh render model.glb --animation walk --frames 0-39:4 --angles 8 --sprite-sheet --out walk.png\n  # renders 10 frames × 8 angles = 80 tiles instead of 320","kind":"task","title":"Add frame step/stride to --frames flag"}	blake3:fr4-X66negVwI7-O99O2_qGh8NNOvCKCYMLwOIn1xA8
1774207698368290	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEtwQE		item.move	bn-2mc	{"state":"done"}	blake3:gGmsHOhlD5J9lpaSIyG9SOQBsslQeWc0Ks2WwI-_zeY
1774208014238200	command-lattice-dev	itc:v3:AWKTkyTOeZ5M5QAAAAAAQa61bvb1ugAAAAEvEQ		item.create	bn-2sl	{"description":"Some AI-generated meshes (TRELLIS) have single-sided faces. When animated, bones can rotate mesh parts so the back faces the camera, causing parts to disappear in the rendered output. The viewer shows them fine (likely has culling disabled or double-sided by default), but the headless renderer culls them.\n\nAdd --no-backface-cull (or --double-sided) flag to disable backface culling during rendering. Alternatively, respect the GLB material's doubleSided property.\n\nRepro: render worker_walk.glb sprite sheet — arm disappears at certain animation frames/angles.","kind":"task","title":"Add --no-backface-cull flag to render"}	blake3:aC6JJKmJGJRhsGMGGtGKtyKvTmu5jDKj5zBWaxWcpmM
1774208092739813	command-lattice-dev	itc:v3:AWKTkyTOeZ5M5QAAAAAAQa61bvb1ugAAAAEvEg		item.create	bn-3op	{"description":"When rendering animated sprite sheets (msh render --animation --frames --angles --sprite-sheet), some sprites have missing arm geometry. The same model and animation plays correctly in the interactive viewer (msh view).\n\nRepro:\n  msh render worker_walk.glb --animation walk --scale 10 --frames 0-39:4 --angles 8 --sprite-sheet --transparent-bg --preset sprite-bake --out walk.png\n\nSome tiles in the output show the arm disappearing. It's not a backface issue (verified with B toggle in viewer — backfaces render red and arm looks fine).\n\nLikely cause: the headless renderer's skinning evaluation produces different results than the viewer at certain frames or camera angles. Could be a timing issue (frame interpolation), a transform ordering issue, or a clipping plane problem at certain angles.","kind":"task","title":"Animated sprite rendering has missing geometry at some frames/angles"}	blake3:nTQkvLwqnki9P0IPsKUVmArcXuFqXw06vKkWlZt1U5E
1774208957512286	command-lattice-dev	itc:v3:AWKTkyTOeZ5M5QAAAAAAQa61bvb1ugAAAAEvEw		item.create	bn-t5n	{"description":"The command-lattice web client uses magenta (255, 0, 255) pixels in sprite sheets as a mask for team color recoloring at load time. AI-generated 3D models don't have magenta regions — they have actual painted colors.\n\nAdd a post-processing pass to msh render --sprite-sheet that identifies a configurable accent color in the rendered output and replaces it with magenta (255, 0, 255). This enables the existing team-color system in the web client to work with AI-generated assets.\n\nUsage:\n  msh render model.glb --sprite-sheet --team-color-mask ff00ff --accent-color <hex> --out sheet.png\n\nWhere --accent-color is the color in the rendered model that should become the team-color-masked region (e.g. the magenta/pink paint on the worker droid). The replacement should use a tolerance/threshold since rendered colors won't be exact due to lighting and shading.\n\nAlternatively, a simpler approach:\n  msh render model.glb --sprite-sheet --team-color-mask --accent-hue 300 --hue-tolerance 30 --out sheet.png\n\nThis would mask any pixel whose hue falls within the specified range, which is more robust against lighting variation.\n\nContext: The worker droid model has rusty magenta/pink accents. These should become the team-color-masked region so different players see their team color. See notes/sprite-integration.md in command-lattice for the full sprite system docs.","kind":"task","title":"Magenta team-color mask pass in sprite sheet rendering"}	blake3:lyXCVQaUBkOASqehJwpdMRXKonF6loK29-sd9ORfk5o
1774209405683889	command-lattice-dev	itc:v3:AWKTkyTOeZ5M5QAAAAAAQa61bvb1ugAAAAEvFA		item.move	bn-t5n	{"state":"done"}	blake3:ebct2SsRccPSQ4c92RsuIHrpeSqn7WV90BNgKvRJuS8
1774220145857030	command-lattice-dev	itc:v3:AWKTkyTOeZ5M5QAAAAAAQa61bvb1ugAAAAEvFQ		item.create	bn-189	{"description":"When rendering sprite sheets with --angles 8, the camera rotates in 45° increments. But AI-generated models (from TRELLIS) don't always face exactly along an axis — they can be rotated a few degrees off.\n\nAdd --angle-offset <degrees> to add a constant rotation offset to all angle steps.\n\nUsage:\n  msh render model.glb --angles 8 --angle-offset 5 --sprite-sheet --out sheet.png\n\nThis rotates the starting angle by 5° (e.g. instead of 0°, 45°, 90°... it renders 5°, 50°, 95°...). Allows compensating for models whose forward direction is slightly off-axis without re-exporting the mesh.","kind":"task","title":"Add --angle-offset to sprite sheet rendering"}	blake3:UOGtV1vuRXytdh2t7GR18BGF0ZL_x3H0lnQ1iHJXOUw
1774220175789298	bob	itc:v3:AWKSc8kyZJPJk5EAAAAAQau9WtV610AAAAEzAQ		item.update	bn-2sl	{"field":"title","value":"NOT DOING | Add --no-backface-cull flag to render"}	blake3:YzIZuAPeACZO8oE0P2IRAW9_aPlHxyf12YQY5YHIygw
1774220175804012	bob	itc:v3:AWKSc8kyZJPJk5EAAAAAQau9WtV610AAAAEzAg		item.update	bn-2sl	{"field":"description","value":"Some AI-generated meshes (TRELLIS) have single-sided faces. When animated, bones can rotate mesh parts so the back faces the camera, causing parts to disappear in the rendered output. The viewer shows them fine (likely has culling disabled or double-sided by default), but the headless renderer culls them.\n\nAdd --no-backface-cull (or --double-sided) flag to disable backface culling during rendering. Alternatively, respect the GLB material's doubleSided property.\n\nRepro: render worker_walk.glb sprite sheet — arm disappears at certain animation frames/angles."}	blake3:IYNC0sS6j5ihNrEl72-3jKl2CfO59v3PoZyNqlvvJq4
