1774962155267666	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEt1AE		item.link	bn-1er	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-3mg"}	blake3:Nr_vvnlQCik_URHte806SFVR7j1X50Z58HSW66o5sRk
1774962179390904	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEt1QE		item.comment	bn-1ao	{"body":"Wrap baby_shark IncrementalDecimator<f32, QuadricError, ...> with min_faces_count. CLI: msh remesh simplify INPUT OUTPUT --target-verts N [--ratio 0.5] [--max-error F]. ~50-100 lines in processing.rs + CLI wiring. If triangle quality is poor, implement custom CollapseStrategy with PaMO's skinny triangle penalty (5.0 * sum(1 - clamp(4*sqrt(3)*area/(e1^2+e2^2+e3^2), 0, 1)))."}	blake3:s7ty4JFE1Q7Pw63AgBatOw1oMp0IXiYBfCmNosWF2tY
1774962179407125	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEt1gE		item.comment	bn-3gh	{"body":"Point-triangle distance with edge/vertex clamping. For a point P and triangle (A,B,C): project P onto plane, check if inside triangle via barycentric coords, if outside clamp to nearest edge or vertex. Return closest point + distance. ~60-80 lines. Reference: Real-Time Collision Detection (Ericson) ch. 5."}	blake3:aSFtNk_pR5zeKw65EDWWgHxLQPbXZZRiHLpSb4wTSt0
1774962179423733	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEt1wE		item.comment	bn-19y	{"body":"Uniform grid spatial hash for O(1) cell lookup. cell_size = 2x average edge length of original mesh. Each cell stores indices of triangles whose AABB overlaps. Query: for a point, check surrounding cells, test each triangle via closest-point-on-triangle. ~80-120 lines. At our mesh sizes (500-4K tris) this is optional — brute force O(n) scan would also work in <1ms — but good practice."}	blake3:8g565vjat0dyxWzrCEFDN6oRy1jZ89tG_csKigVGRzo
1774962179440542	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEt2AE		item.comment	bn-1mu	{"body":"For each interior edge on the original mesh, compute dihedral angle between adjacent face normals. Mark edges where angle < crease_threshold (default 30 deg) as crease edges. Store as Vec<(Vec3, Vec3)> line segments. ~40-60 lines. Uses baby_shark edge_faces() and face_normal() queries."}	blake3:vRV0ZO7FFuAm5zWyohzTwsVhfPA9ec4VG3aZbZd6zw4
1774962179457123	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEt2QE		item.comment	bn-311	{"body":"For a point near a crease edge, project onto the nearest line segment. Standard point-to-segment: t = clamp(dot(P-A, B-A) / dot(B-A, B-A), 0, 1), closest = A + t*(B-A). Apply snap when distance < snap_threshold (default 2x avg edge length). ~30-50 lines."}	blake3:xF1gWtOnqwoqgWjkCIo0abZPmgyeq_az-sexwO2nLtc
1774962179472796	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEt2gE		item.comment	bn-1lz	{"body":"Main iteration: for each vertex, find closest point on original surface (via spatial hash), compute Laplacian (avg neighbors - current pos), blend v_new = v + 0.5*(target-v) + 0.1*laplacian, snap to crease if near. Post-step: check for inverted triangles (normal dot < 0), revert those vertices. 10 iterations default. ~80-120 lines. This is the core of the simplified Stage 3."}	blake3:6GV_zCITkP7D5zTUNyTOmAdMWdsEQHV4S4VndxHK07Y
1774962179488462	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEt2wE		item.comment	bn-3mg	{"body":"Test on actual game asset meshes currently processed by PaMO. Compare: Hausdorff distance to original, triangle quality distribution, visual inspection of sharp edges. Tune alpha/beta/crease_angle/snap_threshold. If quality is poor, escalation path: (1) add skinny triangle penalty to QEM, (2) add O(n^2) intersection post-check, (3) upgrade to gradient descent, (4) full Newton-CG."}	blake3:QrEnIYgdjRSoA_JsrOLDr2TCKMq-6pHiGt6KkmHvOvM
1774962179503247	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEt3AE		item.comment	bn-1er	{"body":"Chain: remesh voxel --method manifold -> remesh simplify --target-verts N -> remesh project --reference original. CLI: msh clean INPUT OUTPUT --target-verts N [--voxel-size F]. ~30 lines wiring. Sensible defaults: voxel-size auto from bounding box, 10 projection iterations."}	blake3:_HwKwavPIpitpcfw7_SJJWGR3UcH8BsyWdNWh_W1lEU
1774962933258603	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEt3QE		item.comment	bn-1ao	{"body":"## Implementation details\n\nLocation: src/mesh/processing.rs (new fn) + src/main.rs (CLI wiring)\n\nAdd 'Simplify' variant to the existing Remesh subcommand enum:\n  remesh simplify INPUT OUTPUT --target-verts N [--ratio 0.5] [--max-error 0.001] [--keep-boundary]\n\nIn processing.rs, add:\n  pub fn remesh_simplify(\n      input: &PathBuf,\n      output: &PathBuf,\n      mesh_name: Option<&str>,\n      target_verts: Option<usize>,\n      ratio: Option<f32>,\n      max_error: Option<f32>,\n      keep_boundary: bool,\n  ) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>>\n\nSteps:\n1. Load mesh via baby_shark io::read_from_file -> CornerTableF\n2. Record initial vertex/face counts\n3. Compute target face count:\n   - If --target-verts N: target_faces = N * 2 (manifold meshes have ~2x faces as verts)\n   - If --ratio R: target_faces = current_faces * R\n   - If both: use whichever is smaller\n4. Build decimator:\n   let mut decimator = IncrementalDecimator::<f32, QuadricError<CornerTableF>, _>::new()\n       .min_faces_count(Some(target_faces))\n       .keep_boundary(keep_boundary);\n   If --max-error given:\n       .decimation_criteria(ConstantErrorDecimationCriteria::new(max_error))\n   Else:\n       .decimation_criteria(AlwaysDecimate)\n5. decimator.decimate(&mut mesh)\n6. Print before/after vertex and face counts\n7. Write output via baby_shark io::write_to_file\n\nbaby_shark imports needed:\n  use baby_shark::decimation::{IncrementalDecimator, QuadricError, AlwaysDecimate, ConstantErrorDecimationCriteria};\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] 'msh remesh simplify cube.obj out.obj --target-verts 50' produces a valid manifold OBJ with ~50 vertices\n- [ ] --ratio 0.5 halves face count (within ~10% tolerance since QEM may stop early for safety)\n- [ ] --max-error prevents collapsing edges above the error threshold\n- [ ] --keep-boundary preserves boundary edges (mesh holes stay open)\n- [ ] Output reports before/after vertex and face counts to stdout\n- [ ] Existing remesh tests still pass\n- [ ] Runs in <100ms on a 4K vertex mesh"}	blake3:b_9k4qgQFBI1ygLzyY19C5qV_8Y5aq8euC_TLcpnTL4
1774962933274870	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEt3gE		item.comment	bn-3gh	{"body":"## Implementation details\n\nLocation: src/mesh/projection.rs (new file)\n\npub fn closest_point_on_triangle(\n    point: &na::Point3<f32>,\n    a: &na::Point3<f32>,\n    b: &na::Point3<f32>,\n    c: &na::Point3<f32>,\n) -> (na::Point3<f32>, f32)\n// Returns (closest_point, squared_distance)\n\nAlgorithm (Ericson, Real-Time Collision Detection, Section 5.1.5):\n1. Compute edge vectors: ab = b-a, ac = c-a, ap = p-a\n2. Check vertex regions via dot products:\n   d1 = dot(ab, ap), d2 = dot(ac, ap)\n   If d1 <= 0 && d2 <= 0: closest = a (vertex region A)\n3. Check edge AB region:\n   bp = p-b, d3 = dot(ab, bp), d4 = dot(ac, bp)\n   If d3 >= 0 && d4 <= d3: closest = b (vertex region B)\n   vc = d1*d4 - d3*d2, if vc <= 0 && d1 >= 0 && d3 <= 0:\n     t = d1/(d1-d3), closest = a + t*ab (edge AB)\n4. Check edge AC region similarly\n5. Check edge BC region similarly\n6. Interior: compute barycentric coords, closest = a + u*ab + v*ac\n\nAlso add a convenience wrapper for mesh queries:\npub fn closest_point_on_mesh(\n    point: &na::Point3<f32>,\n    positions: &[na::Point3<f32>],\n    triangles: &[[u32; 3]],\n) -> (na::Point3<f32>, f32, usize)\n// Returns (closest_point, squared_distance, triangle_index)\n\nThis brute-force version iterates all triangles. The spatial hash (bn-19y) wraps\nthis for acceleration.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Point directly above triangle center returns the projected point with correct distance\n- [ ] Point nearest to edge returns the edge projection, not the face projection\n- [ ] Point nearest to vertex returns the vertex\n- [ ] Degenerate triangles (zero area) don't panic — return nearest vertex\n- [ ] closest_point_on_mesh returns the globally nearest point across all triangles\n- [ ] Unit tests cover all 7 Voronoi regions (3 vertices, 3 edges, 1 interior)\n- [ ] Results match nalgebra's point-triangle distance for spot-check cases"}	blake3:Nk-5rTIX49CSbIbomZgR8hM2ApEViup2XX2yj3y1SI4
1774962933293709	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEt3wE		item.comment	bn-19y	{"body":"## Implementation details\n\nLocation: src/mesh/projection.rs (alongside closest-point code)\n\npub struct SpatialHashGrid {\n    cell_size: f32,\n    cells: HashMap<(i32, i32, i32), Vec<usize>>,  // cell coords -> triangle indices\n    // Cached mesh data for queries\n    positions: Vec<na::Point3<f32>>,\n    triangles: Vec<[u32; 3]>,\n}\n\nimpl SpatialHashGrid {\n    pub fn new(\n        positions: &[na::Point3<f32>],\n        triangles: &[[u32; 3]],\n        cell_size: f32,\n    ) -> Self\n\n    pub fn query_nearest(\n        &self,\n        point: &na::Point3<f32>,\n    ) -> (na::Point3<f32>, f32, usize)\n    // Returns (closest_point, squared_distance, triangle_index)\n}\n\nConstruction:\n1. For each triangle, compute AABB\n2. For each cell the AABB overlaps, insert triangle index\n3. cell_size = 2x average edge length (passed in by caller)\n\nQuery:\n1. Compute cell coords for query point\n2. Search expanding rings of cells (start with 1x1x1, then 3x3x3, etc.)\n3. For each triangle in checked cells, run closest_point_on_triangle\n4. Early termination: once the current best distance < cell_size, no need to expand further\n   (all closer triangles must be in adjacent cells)\n\ncell_key(point) = (floor(x/cell_size), floor(y/cell_size), floor(z/cell_size))\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Query on a point inside the mesh returns distance ~0 to nearest face\n- [ ] Query returns same result as brute-force closest_point_on_mesh for 100 random points\n- [ ] Construction completes in <1ms for 4K triangle mesh\n- [ ] Query completes in <10us per point (vs ~50us brute force at 4K tris)\n- [ ] Empty cells don't waste memory (HashMap-based, not dense array)\n- [ ] Works correctly with meshes not centered at origin\n- [ ] Handles degenerate case: all triangles in same cell (falls back to checking all)"}	blake3:N68Kx006RpzAiumkWjnMcK7FQF92ImbFcbjFcRJjOqA
1774962933319971	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEt4AE		item.comment	bn-1mu	{"body":"## Implementation details\n\nLocation: src/mesh/projection.rs\n\npub fn detect_crease_edges(\n    positions: &[na::Point3<f32>],\n    triangles: &[[u32; 3]],\n    crease_angle_deg: f32,\n) -> Vec<[na::Point3<f32>; 2]>\n// Returns list of crease edge line segments as [endpoint_a, endpoint_b]\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Build edge -> adjacent faces map:\n   HashMap<(u32, u32), Vec<usize>> where edge key = (min(v0,v1), max(v0,v1))\n   Each triangle contributes 3 edges.\n2. For each edge with exactly 2 adjacent faces (interior edge):\n   a. Compute face normals n1, n2\n   b. dihedral_angle = acos(clamp(dot(n1, n2), -1, 1))\n   c. If dihedral_angle > (180 - crease_angle_deg).to_radians():\n      This is a sharp crease. Add [positions[v0], positions[v1]] to result.\n\nNote: dihedral angle convention — the angle between face normals is the supplement\nof the dihedral angle. Two coplanar faces have normal dot = 1.0 (0 deg between\nnormals = 180 deg dihedral). A 90-degree crease has normal dot = 0.0. So the\nthreshold is: dot(n1, n2) < cos((180 - crease_angle_deg).to_radians()) which\nsimplifies to dot(n1, n2) < -cos(crease_angle_deg.to_radians()).\n\nWait, let's be precise:\n- crease_angle_deg = 30 means 'edges sharper than 30 degrees from flat'\n- 'flat' = 180 deg dihedral = normals pointing same direction (dot = 1.0)\n- 30 deg from flat = 150 deg dihedral = normals at 30 deg apart (dot = cos(30) ≈ 0.866)\n- So threshold: dot(n1, n2) < cos(crease_angle_deg.to_radians())\n\nThat's the correct formulation: lower dot product = sharper edge.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A cube (all 90-degree edges) with crease_angle=45 detects all 12 edges\n- [ ] A cube with crease_angle=91 detects no edges (90 < 91, so not 'sharper than 91')\n- [ ] A sphere (all smooth) with crease_angle=30 detects no edges\n- [ ] Boundary edges (only 1 adjacent face) are excluded\n- [ ] Non-manifold edges (3+ adjacent faces) are excluded\n- [ ] Returns empty vec for an empty mesh without panicking\n- [ ] Unit test with known dihedral angles validates the threshold math"}	blake3:uYlHSMg3HbokBP7ByuhjQP8wdXRQW5k5PjxLz1ewIVc
1774962933342270	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEt4QE		item.comment	bn-311	{"body":"## Implementation details\n\nLocation: src/mesh/projection.rs\n\npub fn snap_to_nearest_crease(\n    point: &na::Point3<f32>,\n    crease_edges: &[[na::Point3<f32>; 2]],\n    snap_threshold: f32,\n) -> Option<na::Point3<f32>>\n// Returns Some(snapped_point) if within threshold, None otherwise\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. For each crease edge segment [A, B]:\n   a. t = clamp(dot(P-A, B-A) / dot(B-A, B-A), 0.0, 1.0)\n   b. closest = A + t * (B - A)\n   c. dist_sq = ||P - closest||^2\n2. Find the crease edge with minimum distance\n3. If sqrt(min_dist_sq) < snap_threshold: return Some(closest_on_min_edge)\n4. Else: return None\n\nFor performance with many crease edges, could also put them in a spatial hash.\nAt our mesh sizes (<500 crease edges typically), brute force is fine.\n\nAlso add a convenience function for the projection loop:\npub fn find_nearest_crease(\n    point: &na::Point3<f32>,\n    crease_edges: &[[na::Point3<f32>; 2]],\n) -> (na::Point3<f32>, f32)\n// Returns (closest_point_on_any_crease, distance)\n// Always returns a result; caller decides whether to snap based on threshold.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Point on a crease edge returns that exact point (within f32 epsilon)\n- [ ] Point between two crease edges snaps to the nearer one\n- [ ] Point beyond snap_threshold returns None\n- [ ] Point nearest to endpoint of crease segment returns that endpoint\n- [ ] Works with zero-length crease edges (degenerate) — returns the single point\n- [ ] Empty crease_edges vec returns None without panicking"}	blake3:vKSWP32RHkmtxXs3WPa-x8tN1aG2-oCBQy2mJR2UKu8
1774962933358338	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEt4gE		item.comment	bn-1lz	{"body":"## Implementation details\n\nLocation: src/mesh/projection.rs (pub fn project_to_reference)\n\npub fn project_to_reference(\n    simplified_positions: &mut [na::Point3<f32>],\n    simplified_triangles: &[[u32; 3]],\n    reference_grid: &SpatialHashGrid,\n    crease_edges: &[[na::Point3<f32>; 2]],\n    config: &ProjectionConfig,\n) -> ProjectionStats\n\npub struct ProjectionConfig {\n    pub iterations: usize,         // default 10\n    pub alpha: f32,                // projection weight, default 0.5\n    pub beta: f32,                 // Laplacian weight, default 0.1\n    pub snap_threshold: f32,       // crease snap distance, default 2 * avg_edge_len\n}\n\npub struct ProjectionStats {\n    pub iterations_run: usize,\n    pub avg_displacement: f32,      // average vertex movement from start\n    pub max_displacement: f32,      // max vertex movement from start\n    pub inversions_reverted: usize, // total triangle inversions caught\n    pub vertices_snapped: usize,    // vertices snapped to creases\n}\n\nAlgorithm per iteration:\n1. Save current positions as old_positions\n2. Build vertex -> adjacent faces map (once, before loop)\n3. Build vertex -> neighbor vertices map (once, before loop, for Laplacian)\n4. For each vertex v (index i):\n   a. target = reference_grid.query_nearest(v).0\n   b. laplacian = (sum of neighbor positions / num_neighbors) - v\n   c. v_new = v + alpha * (target - v) + beta * laplacian\n   d. if let Some(snapped) = snap_to_nearest_crease(v_new, crease_edges, snap_threshold):\n        v_new = snapped\n   e. simplified_positions[i] = v_new\n5. Inversion check:\n   For each triangle in simplified_triangles:\n     old_normal = compute_normal(old_positions, tri)\n     new_normal = compute_normal(simplified_positions, tri)\n     if dot(old_normal, new_normal) < 0.0:\n       revert all 3 vertex positions to old_positions\n       inversions_reverted += 1\n6. Convergence check (optional): if max vertex movement < epsilon, break early\n\nKey detail: vertex adjacency and neighbor maps are built from simplified_triangles,\nnot from the reference mesh. The simplified mesh topology stays fixed; only\npositions move.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A cube simplified to fewer verts, then projected back to original cube, has vertices on cube faces (Hausdorff dist < voxel_size)\n- [ ] Sharp edges of the cube are preserved via crease snapping\n- [ ] No inverted triangles in output (all normals consistent with input orientation)\n- [ ] Convergence: average displacement decreases monotonically across iterations\n- [ ] 10 iterations on 2K vertex mesh completes in <50ms\n- [ ] Stats output shows meaningful numbers (displacement, snapped count, etc.)\n- [ ] Zero iterations returns the mesh unchanged\n- [ ] Works when crease_edges is empty (pure surface projection, no snapping)\n- [ ] Boundary vertices (if mesh has holes) are still projected but not Laplacian-smoothed into the interior"}	blake3:DvlcosLDWcY0AIUqRDusYvqoBI-CvNgON5s0JhQgCY8
1774962933374063	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEt4wE		item.comment	bn-1yl	{"body":"## Implementation details\n\nLocation: src/main.rs (CLI) + src/mesh/processing.rs (orchestration fn)\n\nAdd 'Project' variant to the Remesh subcommand enum:\n  remesh project INPUT OUTPUT --reference ORIGINAL [--iterations 10] [--alpha 0.5] [--beta 0.1] [--crease-angle 30] [--snap-threshold F]\n\nIn processing.rs, add:\n  pub fn remesh_project(\n      input: &PathBuf,       // simplified mesh\n      output: &PathBuf,\n      reference: &PathBuf,   // original mesh\n      mesh_name: Option<&str>,\n      config: ProjectionConfig,\n      crease_angle_deg: f32,\n  ) -> Result<ProjectionStats, Box<dyn std::error::Error>>\n\nSteps:\n1. Load simplified mesh (input) -> MeshWithColors -> extract positions + triangles\n2. Load reference mesh (reference) -> extract positions + triangles\n3. Compute avg edge length of reference mesh\n4. Build SpatialHashGrid from reference mesh (cell_size = 2 * avg_edge_len)\n5. Detect crease edges on reference mesh (crease_angle_deg)\n6. Set snap_threshold = 2 * avg_edge_len if not specified\n7. Run project_to_reference(...)\n8. Print stats (displacement, inversions, snapped vertices)\n9. Write output OBJ\n\nInput format support: OBJ and GLB for both simplified and reference meshes.\nOutput format: OBJ (baby_shark write_to_file).\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] 'msh remesh project simplified.obj out.obj --reference original.obj' runs and produces valid OBJ\n- [ ] --iterations 0 produces identical output to input (passthrough)\n- [ ] --crease-angle 0 disables crease detection (no snapping)\n- [ ] Stats printed to stdout: iterations, avg displacement, max displacement, inversions reverted, vertices snapped\n- [ ] Errors gracefully if reference file doesn't exist\n- [ ] Errors if simplified mesh has more vertices than reference (likely swapped args)\n- [ ] Both OBJ and GLB input supported for reference mesh\n- [ ] Help text explains what --reference means and the typical pipeline"}	blake3:Dp_r6-uPC_QYU_vaH6mOoz4VGo4nzy6ADD8fs2DbTZU
1774962933393713	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEt5AE		item.comment	bn-3mg	{"body":"## Implementation details\n\nLocation: evaluation notes in notes/eval-mesh-cleanup-quality.md\n\nTest meshes: use the game asset meshes currently processed by tools/pamo-clean.\nRun both pipelines on the same inputs and compare.\n\nPipeline A (PaMO):\n  tools/pamo-clean input.obj pamo-output.obj --ratio 0.5\n\nPipeline B (msh native):\n  msh remesh voxel input.obj stage1.obj --method manifold --voxel-size 0.01\n  msh remesh simplify stage1.obj stage2.obj --target-verts N\n  msh remesh project stage2.obj msh-output.obj --reference input.obj\n\nMetrics to compare:\n1. Hausdorff distance to original (msh stats or meshlab)\n2. Mean distance to original\n3. Triangle quality histogram: min angle distribution, aspect ratio\n4. Visual inspection in msh view: sharp edges, smooth regions, artifacts\n5. Vertex count of output (both should hit similar targets)\n6. Runtime (PaMO GPU vs msh CPU)\n\nParameters to tune:\n- voxel_size: try 0.01, 0.005, auto-from-bbox\n- projection iterations: try 5, 10, 20, 50\n- alpha: try 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\n- beta: try 0.0, 0.05, 0.1, 0.2\n- crease_angle: try 20, 30, 45, 60\n- snap_threshold: try 1x, 2x, 3x avg edge length\n\nEscalation triggers (quality insufficient):\n1. Skinny triangles > 10% of total -> add skinny penalty to QEM (custom CollapseStrategy)\n2. Self-intersections detected -> add O(n^2) tri-tri intersection post-check\n3. Hausdorff > 2x PaMO output -> try gradient descent upgrade to projection\n4. Sharp features still lost -> try lower alpha, more iterations, smaller crease_angle\n\nDocument results and parameter choices in the eval notes file.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] At least 5 representative meshes tested (varying complexity, sharp vs smooth)\n- [ ] Hausdorff distance within 2x of PaMO output for all test meshes\n- [ ] No self-intersections in any output\n- [ ] Visual inspection: no obviously worse sharp edges vs PaMO\n- [ ] Default parameters chosen and documented\n- [ ] Runtime < 1 second for all test meshes (CPU)\n- [ ] Escalation path documented if any metric fails\n- [ ] Results written to notes/eval-mesh-cleanup-quality.md with before/after screenshots or measurements"}	blake3:ueKgrDErRttTHzlOB5UIHpO_nqXFE0fVR7VhsM0WwN0
1774962933427604	msh-dev	itc:v3:AWLzMzyc5k_JEAAAAAAAQfbb13a_UAAAAAEt5QE		item.comment	bn-1er	{"body":"## Implementation details\n\nLocation: src/main.rs (new top-level command) + src/mesh/processing.rs\n\nNew top-level command (not a remesh subcommand):\n  msh clean INPUT OUTPUT --target-verts N [--voxel-size F] [--iterations 10] [--crease-angle 30] [--no-fix] [--no-project]\n\nIn processing.rs:\n  pub fn clean_mesh(\n      input: &PathBuf,\n      output: &PathBuf,\n      mesh_name: Option<&str>,\n      target_verts: usize,\n      voxel_size: Option<f32>,   // auto if None\n      projection_config: ProjectionConfig,\n      crease_angle_deg: f32,\n      skip_fix: bool,\n      skip_project: bool,\n  ) -> Result<CleanStats, Box<dyn std::error::Error>>\n\nPipeline steps:\n1. Load original mesh, save reference copy in memory\n2. If !skip_fix:\n   a. Compute voxel_size if not specified: bbox_max_dim / 128\n   b. Run voxel remesh (manifold method) -> stage1 mesh\n3. Run QEM simplify to target_verts -> stage2 mesh\n4. If !skip_project:\n   a. Build spatial hash from reference mesh\n   b. Detect crease edges on reference mesh\n   c. Run projection loop -> final mesh\n5. Write output\n6. Print pipeline summary: original verts -> remeshed verts -> simplified verts -> projected verts\n\nAuto voxel size logic:\n- Compute bounding box max dimension D\n- voxel_size = D / 128 (gives ~128^3 effective resolution)\n- This matches PaMO's adaptive resolution for small meshes\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] 'msh clean input.obj output.obj --target-verts 1000' runs full 3-stage pipeline\n- [ ] --no-fix skips voxel remeshing (for already-manifold meshes)\n- [ ] --no-project skips projection (for when you just want simplification)\n- [ ] Auto voxel size produces reasonable results without manual tuning\n- [ ] Pipeline summary printed: vertex counts at each stage\n- [ ] Output is manifold (no boundary edges) when input was non-manifold\n- [ ] Total runtime < 2 seconds for typical 2-4K vertex mesh\n- [ ] Errors gracefully at each stage with clear message about which stage failed\n- [ ] Works with OBJ and GLB input"}	blake3:1V1T8Iu0un74jotwyYL1aETof7kpimuy6RjxqQmGMnE
